{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: 10,000 more police and completion of 20,000 CSOs; anti‑social behaviour tools and tougher community sentences; create a UK Border Force; maintain a minimum nuclear deterrent; EU reforms subject to a referendum; protect jury trial and tighten judicial control of any control orders. TEXT_C: Decommission nuclear weapons and reduce defence spending; oppose national ID cards; treat drug use as a health issue; extensive migrants’ and asylum rights (allow work/benefits, ratify UN Migrant Workers Convention); fully proportional representation and a written constitution; strong environmentalism and localisation; withdraw troops from Iraq and prioritise diplomacy.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate skews TAN, valuing order, national identity, and security; TEXT_D includes law‑and‑order expansion, a dedicated border force, and retention of a nuclear deterrent, and is more cautious on supranational authority via referendum, which align better with TAN commitments. TEXT_C argues consistently from GAL premises—civil liberties, de‑militarisation, expansive migrant rights, and strong environmentalism—placing it further from the TAN‑leaning profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
